#! ia 1.0

@decision public-catalogue-cut
  meaning
    says "inventarch/marketplace, catalogue name inventarch, is the public InventArch plugin catalogue. It was cut on 2026-10-30 from the tree of InventArch's internal plugin catalogue at dd993c0, with fresh history: the first commit imports that tree unchanged so the removal is reviewable, and the next removes everything that is not public. It ships one plugin, the inventarch core, and the public kits ia-method, ia-kb and ia-authoring."
  work
    title "Cut the public catalogue with fresh history"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What is this repository, and where did it come from?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "inventarch/marketplace, catalogue name inventarch, is the public InventArch plugin catalogue. It was cut on 2026-10-30 from the tree of InventArch's internal plugin catalogue at dd993c0, with fresh history: the first commit imports that tree unchanged so the removal is reviewable, and the next removes everything that is not public. It ships one plugin, the inventarch core, and the public kits ia-method, ia-kb and ia-authoring."
    rationale "Public kits and their install machinery need a home anyone can read; proprietary methods stay in private sources and reach agents through an InventArch account."

@decision public-apache-license
  meaning
    says "Apache-2.0 for every file here, with LICENSE and NOTICE copied from inventarch/ia; every plugin manifest and kit descriptor says Apache-2.0."
  work
    title "License everything public under Apache-2.0"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Under which licence do the public plugin and kits ship?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Apache-2.0 for every file here, with LICENSE and NOTICE copied from inventarch/ia; every plugin manifest and kit descriptor says Apache-2.0."
    rationale "It matches the public IA framework the kits build on."

@decision public-kit-naming
  meaning
    says "Every kit: folder ia-<n>, kit id inventarch/ia-<n>-kit, main system ia-<n>, workspace ia-<n>-kit, distribution ia-<n>-distribution, steward ia-<n>-steward; a kit with several systems names the extra ones ia-<n>-<part>. Public or private is shown by the repository that holds a kit, never by its name."
  work
    title "Name every kit ia-<name>"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How are kits named?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Every kit: folder ia-<n>, kit id inventarch/ia-<n>-kit, main system ia-<n>, workspace ia-<n>-kit, distribution ia-<n>-distribution, steward ia-<n>-steward; a kit with several systems names the extra ones ia-<n>-<part>. Public or private is shown by the repository that holds a kit, never by its name."
    rationale "One pattern makes a kit's records predictable from its name, and moving a kit between repositories never renames it."

@decision public-default-kits
  meaning
    says "The core bundles exactly three kits: ia-method for Work (the operating loop, the provisional-foundation ceiling, and one method capability with a four-cell playbook per public record word, governance words read-and-apply only), ia-kb for Knowledge (placement, binding, maintenance, checks, migration and review, with the kb-workspace-location convention), and ia-authoring for Delivery (record, host and catalogue authoring as one process). The retired ids inventarch/host-authoring-kit and inventarch/catalogue-authoring-kit are replaced by ia-authoring."
  work
    title "Carry ia-method, ia-kb and ia-authoring as the lane defaults"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which kits does the public core carry, and which lane does each cover?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The core bundles exactly three kits: ia-method for Work (the operating loop, the provisional-foundation ceiling, and one method capability with a four-cell playbook per public record word, governance words read-and-apply only), ia-kb for Knowledge (placement, binding, maintenance, checks, migration and review, with the kb-workspace-location convention), and ia-authoring for Delivery (record, host and catalogue authoring as one process). The retired ids inventarch/host-authoring-kit and inventarch/catalogue-authoring-kit are replaced by ia-authoring."
    rationale "Each lane needs a public default; host and catalogue authoring must be one consistent process with record authoring."

@decision public-opal-per-lane
  meaning
    says "Each lane's entry capability (work.*, knowledge.*, delivery.*) is declared whole in its kit's delivery.json, so its packet carries all four cells; knowledge place maps to the plan phase; per-activity keys keep their one-phase scope keys, and --phase overrides. No word is minted: phases are values of the public phase word."
  work
    title "Make each lane entry a whole four-phase procedure"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How is the orient, plan, act, learn loop represented in each lane?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "Each lane's entry capability (work.*, knowledge.*, delivery.*) is declared whole in its kit's delivery.json, so its packet carries all four cells; knowledge place maps to the plan phase; per-activity keys keep their one-phase scope keys, and --phase overrides. No word is minted: phases are values of the public phase word."
    rationale "A request with no specific activity gets the whole loop; a specific activity gets the cell it needs."

@decision public-releases-format-2
  meaning
    says "releases.json is a small index (format 2: kit, latest, and one row per release naming releases/<version>.json where its records were); each per-version file holds that release's record names and is immutable once published. Conversion is lossless (expanding the index rebuilds format 1 byte for byte), the consistency guard compares both sides through one expand(), new rows add source {repository, commit}, and the history is left out of the implementation digest and the method closure capture."
  work
    title "Split kit release history per version"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How are a kit's releases recorded?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "releases.json is a small index (format 2: kit, latest, and one row per release naming releases/<version>.json where its records were); each per-version file holds that release's record names and is immutable once published. Conversion is lossless (expanding the index rebuilds format 1 byte for byte), the consistency guard compares both sides through one expand(), new rows add source {repository, commit}, and the history is left out of the implementation digest and the method closure capture."
    rationale "The embedded record lists made one file tens of thousands of lines long and changed every pin at every release."

@decision public-kit-lookup
  meaning
    says "The core resolves a kit from its own config/kits, then from <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/, filled by scripts/kit.mjs add --from <folder or archive> today and by the InventArch API later. The user verbs are list, add, bind, upgrade, repin, rebind and check in plugins/ia/scripts/kit.mjs; the maintainer verbs are pack, bundle, index and convert in tools/kit.mjs; every write validates first and previews unless --apply. kits/index.json broadcasts each kit's latest release and the binding keys it offers."
  work
    title "Look kits up in the plugin, then in the IA home"
    status superseded
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where does the core find a kit?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "The core resolves a kit from its own config/kits, then from <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/, filled by scripts/kit.mjs add --from <folder or archive> today and by the InventArch API later. The user verbs are list, add, bind, upgrade, repin, rebind and check in plugins/ia/scripts/kit.mjs; the maintainer verbs are pack, bundle, index and convert in tools/kit.mjs; every write validates first and previews unless --apply. kits/index.json broadcasts each kit's latest release and the binding keys it offers."
    rationale "Kits served outside the plugin need an install location the resolver trusts, and repositories need one tool to upgrade, repin and rebind after a release."

@decision public-account-offer
  meaning
    says "At session startup only, once per session, never blocking, in one short message, the core tells the user how to add the remote InventArch MCP server (https://api.inventarch.dev/mcp); the host runs OAuth with PKCE, and the plugin stores no token and makes no network call. An account counts as present when the host already lists an inventarch MCP server in its documented local configuration or a design binding is set in the environment. The offer is suppressed by 'accountOffer': false in ~/.ia/config.json, by IA_NO_ACCOUNT_OFFER=1, or by a non-empty CI."
  work
    title "Offer an InventArch account once per session"
    status superseded
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the public core offer an account?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "At session startup only, once per session, never blocking, in one short message, the core tells the user how to add the remote InventArch MCP server (https://api.inventarch.dev/mcp); the host runs OAuth with PKCE, and the plugin stores no token and makes no network call. An account counts as present when the host already lists an inventarch MCP server in its documented local configuration or a design binding is set in the environment. The offer is suppressed by 'accountOffer': false in ~/.ia/config.json, by IA_NO_ACCOUNT_OFFER=1, or by a non-empty CI."
    rationale "Subscription kits need an account, and the offer must never get in the way of work."

@decision public-governance-probe
  meaning
    says "When .ia/governance.json exists and no installed kit declares governance handlers (a tool pre handler in its handlers.json), the session hook says once, at startup, that the repository is governed and to add the ia-governance kit (ia kit add). No plugin is looked for. It never falls back silently to ungoverned work."
  work
    title "Say when a governed repository lacks its governance kit"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What does the public core do in a repository adopted for governance?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "When .ia/governance.json exists and no installed kit declares governance handlers (a tool pre handler in its handlers.json), the session hook says once, at startup, that the repository is governed and to add the ia-governance kit (ia kit add). No plugin is looked for. It never falls back silently to ungoverned work."
    rationale "Governance is not part of the public offering, and pretending a governed repository is ungoverned would be worse than saying so."

@decision public-workspace-placement
  meaning
    says "User-facing workspaces default to ~/Documents/ia/<domain> (studio, music, design and so on), and the user can point elsewhere. ~/.ia is the IA home only: config, state, the location registry and installed kits. A knowledge repository holds one .ia/src: a personal or single-team KB has one @workspace; an organisation store has one @workspace per department, each declaring its scope folders, plus an organisation @workspace that also declares the repository root (. @authored); a workspaces/<name>/ folder is only for a workspace released or adopted on its own."
  work
    title "Place user workspaces under ~/Documents/ia"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where do a user's workspaces live?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "User-facing workspaces default to ~/Documents/ia/<domain> (studio, music, design and so on), and the user can point elsewhere. ~/.ia is the IA home only: config, state, the location registry and installed kits. A knowledge repository holds one .ia/src: a personal or single-team KB has one @workspace; an organisation store has one @workspace per department, each declaring its scope folders, plus an organisation @workspace that also declares the repository root (. @authored); a workspaces/<name>/ folder is only for a workspace released or adopted on its own."
    rationale "The CLI refuses a workspace inside its home, and a predictable default keeps workspaces easy to find. The knowledge-repository layout is the operator's ruling dec-132 (2026-10-31), which replaced the earlier one-workspace-at-the-root wording."

@decision public-plugin-components
  meaning
    says "Skills, agents, references, templates, examples, config, node scripts and tests. No LSP servers, commands, settings, monitors or bin/, no MCP server but the core's IA door (public-door-mcp), and no hooks, mcpServers or permissionMode in skill or agent frontmatter. At most four model-invocable skills per plugin; stage-skill descriptions at most 160 characters; every agent declares a tools allowlist. The only hooks are the core's qualified set (public-core-hooks)."
  work
    title "Admit only reviewable plugin components"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What may a plugin in this catalogue ship?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "Skills, agents, references, templates, examples, config, node scripts and tests. No LSP servers, commands, settings, monitors or bin/, no MCP server but the core's IA door (public-door-mcp), and no hooks, mcpServers or permissionMode in skill or agent frontmatter. At most four model-invocable skills per plugin; stage-skill descriptions at most 160 characters; every agent declares a tools allowlist. The only hooks are the core's qualified set (public-core-hooks)."
    rationale "A Git install copies a plugin's whole folder, so everything that ships must be reviewable text with no ambient authority."

@decision public-core-hooks
  meaning
    says "The SessionStart bootstrap (startup, resume and compact, scripts/bootstrap.mjs, 15 seconds) prints the workspace identity and the resolve instruction, makes the account offer at startup and the governance probe, re-injects the current task pin after a compaction, reads no credentials and never fetches. scripts/handlers.mjs runs the handlers installed kits declare, with one event argument: SessionStart for every source (60 seconds), UserPromptSubmit (30), PreToolUse (30) and PostToolUse (60) on Write, Edit, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit, Bash and PowerShell, and Stop (30). tools/validate.py pins all of them to exactly these events, matchers, commands and timeouts, and the Codex manifest keeps them off."
  work
    title "Pin the core's hooks"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which hooks does the core ship, and how are they held?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "The SessionStart bootstrap (startup, resume and compact, scripts/bootstrap.mjs, 15 seconds) prints the workspace identity and the resolve instruction, makes the account offer at startup and the governance probe, re-injects the current task pin after a compaction, reads no credentials and never fetches. scripts/handlers.mjs runs the handlers installed kits declare, with one event argument: SessionStart for every source (60 seconds), UserPromptSubmit (30), PreToolUse (30) and PostToolUse (60) on Write, Edit, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit, Bash and PowerShell, and Stop (30). tools/validate.py pins all of them to exactly these events, matchers, commands and timeouts, and the Codex manifest keeps them off."
    rationale "Hooks run without a prompt, so each one is narrow, read-only and pinned."

@decision public-codex-manifest-interface
  meaning
    says "A Codex manifest is the Claude manifest without displayName and dependencies, plus skills and Codex's interface block (displayName, shortDescription, category); a hook plugin adds the empty hook configuration that keeps its hooks off in Codex. Every plugin ships a CODEX.md, and every skill opens with a bridge line to it."
  work
    title "Derive the Codex manifest from the Claude one"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How do the two host manifests relate?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A Codex manifest is the Claude manifest without displayName and dependencies, plus skills and Codex's interface block (displayName, shortDescription, category); a hook plugin adds the empty hook configuration that keeps its hooks off in Codex. Every plugin ships a CODEX.md, and every skill opens with a bridge line to it."
    rationale "Codex reads display fields from the interface block and lists every skill."

@decision public-local-gates
  meaning
    says "npm ci installs lefthook, Biome and the pinned @inventarch/cli; lefthook runs validate, selection --check, bundle --check, consistency, lint and the language check before each commit, and again before each push with consistency --base origin/main. CI runs the same and the test suites."
  work
    title "Run the gates before every commit and push"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which checks guard this repository, and where do they run?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "npm ci installs lefthook, Biome and the pinned @inventarch/cli; lefthook runs validate, selection --check, bundle --check, consistency, lint and the language check before each commit, and again before each push with consistency --base origin/main. CI runs the same and the test suites."
    rationale "Problems are cheapest to fix before they leave the machine."

@decision public-ci-matrix
  meaning
    says "Ubuntu for every pull request into main; Ubuntu, Windows and macOS for every push to main, including the native qualification shards."
  work
    title "Run CI on Ubuntu for pull requests and on three systems for main"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "On which operating systems does CI run?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "Ubuntu for every pull request into main; Ubuntu, Windows and macOS for every push to main, including the native qualification shards."
    rationale "Path and line-ending bugs show up on Windows and macOS; running them on main keeps pull requests fast."

@decision public-core-1-0-0
  meaning
    says "inventarch 1.0.0: the public reset changes the licence, the repository, the kit set (private kits removed, two authoring kit ids retired), the release format and the hooks' behaviour, so it is a breaking release under a fresh history."
  work
    title "Release the public core as 1.0.0"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which version does the first public core carry?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "inventarch 1.0.0: the public reset changes the licence, the repository, the kit set (private kits removed, two authoring kit ids retired), the release format and the hooks' behaviour, so it is a breaking release under a fresh history."
    rationale "A repository bound to a kit the public core no longer carries must notice the change; a major version says so."

@decision public-plugin-ia
  meaning
    says "The plugin is ia (ia@inventarch; the catalogue stays inventarch, and kit ids do not change). Its model-invocable entry skills are the three lanes, ia:work, ia:knowledge and ia:delivery; the setup skill is the user-invoked ia:init, merged with the CLI-rendered plugin's init steps, beside a user-invoked ia:doctor; its agents are ia:reader, ia:checker and ia:worker; a capability bound to a lane is shown as ia-<lane>:<activity>, the binding key re-spelled, whichever kit supplies it (a lane's entry, <lane>.*, reads ia-<lane>). Until the CLI changes so that ia host adds this marketplace and installs ia@inventarch from it, users remove the CLI-rendered registration of the same catalogue name (claude plugin marketplace remove inventarch) before adding this one."
  work
    title "Name the one public plugin ia, with three lanes"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What is the public plugin called, and which skills does it advertise?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The plugin is ia (ia@inventarch; the catalogue stays inventarch, and kit ids do not change). Its model-invocable entry skills are the three lanes, ia:work, ia:knowledge and ia:delivery; the setup skill is the user-invoked ia:init, merged with the CLI-rendered plugin's init steps, beside a user-invoked ia:doctor; its agents are ia:reader, ia:checker and ia:worker; a capability bound to a lane is shown as ia-<lane>:<activity>, the binding key re-spelled, whichever kit supplies it (a lane's entry, <lane>.*, reads ia-<lane>). Until the CLI changes so that ia host adds this marketplace and installs ia@inventarch from it, users remove the CLI-rendered registration of the same catalogue name (claude plugin marketplace remove inventarch) before adding this one."
    rationale "Everything a person types or reads starts with ia, and one plugin with three lanes is what the public offering is."

@decision public-door-mcp
  meaning
    says "The core ships one MCP server, the IA door, as plugins/ia/.mcp.json: server ia, run as node scripts/door.mjs, a launcher that starts npx -y @inventarch/mcp-door at the plugin's IA pin (lib/cli.mjs PINNED, set by the current CLI pin decision) never through a shell (on Windows, where npx is a .cmd shim, it runs npm's npx-cli.js with node), with stdio passed through and IA_KITS_DIR set to the plugin's config/kits. Codex reads a plugin's .mcp.json too, so the Codex manifest keeps the door off with an empty mcpServers until it is qualified there. Claude Code names its tools mcp__plugin_ia_ia__<tool>, and the reader, checker and worker allowlists name ia_session, ia_catalogue and ia_method that way (reader and worker also ia_position, ia_read and ia_vocabulary). The lanes call ia_session once per session, ia_catalogue {lane} to choose a key and ia_method {key} for the packet; the lanes use only the door's tools, with no script fallback (task drop-door-fallback, done). tools/validate.py admits that one file, exactly."
  work
    title "Ship the IA door as the core's MCP server"
    status superseded
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the core reach the method catalogue and packets without a per-workspace registration?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The core ships one MCP server, the IA door, as plugins/ia/.mcp.json: server ia, run as node scripts/door.mjs, a launcher that starts npx -y @inventarch/mcp-door at the plugin's IA pin (lib/cli.mjs PINNED, set by the current CLI pin decision) never through a shell (on Windows, where npx is a .cmd shim, it runs npm's npx-cli.js with node), with stdio passed through and IA_KITS_DIR set to the plugin's config/kits. Codex reads a plugin's .mcp.json too, so the Codex manifest keeps the door off with an empty mcpServers until it is qualified there. Claude Code names its tools mcp__plugin_ia_ia__<tool>, and the reader, checker and worker allowlists name ia_session, ia_catalogue and ia_method that way (reader and worker also ia_position, ia_read and ia_vocabulary). The lanes call ia_session once per session, ia_catalogue {lane} to choose a key and ia_method {key} for the packet; the lanes use only the door's tools, with no script fallback (task drop-door-fallback, done). tools/validate.py admits that one file, exactly."
    rationale "One door carries every method for every host; launching it from the plugin needs no local path and no second server, and pinning it like the CLI keeps it reviewable."

@decision public-kit-handlers
  meaning
    says "A kit may ship handlers.json (format 1: session handlers with their events, prompt, tool with phase pre or post and a tool-name match, and stop, each a .mjs script inside the kit with a timeout of at most 25 seconds). scripts/handlers.mjs runs them from SessionStart (every source), UserPromptSubmit, PreToolUse and PostToolUse on edits and shell commands, and Stop, only for installed kits (this plugin's config/kits and <IA home>/kits) whose folder holds the script, so a fetched release, which carries no executables, never runs one. Each runs as node <script> with the hook JSON on stdin and IA_KIT_ROOT, IA_WORKSPACE_ROOT and IA_HOME set; the core merges their additionalContext at most 2,000 characters per kit; a pre block is honoured only in a workspace with .ia/governance.json; with no handler declared every hook is a silent no-op. onwrite.json runs as tool post handlers. handlers.json is a sidecar the sources digest covers, and its scripts must be declared assets. A run may also be the one form ${PLUGIN}/scripts/method-advise.mjs, the core's own allowlisted script, for a kit that ships no code (decision public-method-advisory-run-points)."
  work
    title "Run the handlers installed kits declare"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does a kit run session, prompt, tool and stop logic, such as a governance ledger, without a plugin of its own?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A kit may ship handlers.json (format 1: session handlers with their events, prompt, tool with phase pre or post and a tool-name match, and stop, each a .mjs script inside the kit with a timeout of at most 25 seconds). scripts/handlers.mjs runs them from SessionStart (every source), UserPromptSubmit, PreToolUse and PostToolUse on edits and shell commands, and Stop, only for installed kits (this plugin's config/kits and <IA home>/kits) whose folder holds the script, so a fetched release, which carries no executables, never runs one. Each runs as node <script> with the hook JSON on stdin and IA_KIT_ROOT, IA_WORKSPACE_ROOT and IA_HOME set; the core merges their additionalContext at most 2,000 characters per kit; a pre block is honoured only in a workspace with .ia/governance.json; with no handler declared every hook is a silent no-op. onwrite.json runs as tool post handlers. handlers.json is a sidecar the sources digest covers, and its scripts must be declared assets. A run may also be the one form ${PLUGIN}/scripts/method-advise.mjs, the core's own allowlisted script, for a kit that ships no code (decision public-method-advisory-run-points)."
    rationale "Governance, Studio and work engines become kit scripts, so the public core runs them and no private plugin is needed; opting in per workspace keeps a kit from blocking work nobody asked it to govern."

@decision public-kit-dependencies
  meaning
    says "A kit release's .ia/release.json dependencies other than inventarch/language name a kit id and range. Adoption (kit.mjs add), add --from and the restored read root resolve each dependency, transitively and dependencies first, from the kits this plugin carries, then <IA home>/kits, then the dependency's own folder next to the kit; the first whose latest release satisfies the range wins. Its sources must pack to that recorded release, and its archive goes with the language base into the copy's or the workspace's vendor catalog, so ia install --catalog and ia restore --catalog resolve it. A missing dependency, a cycle or two releases of one kit is refused before anything is written."
  work
    title "Vendor the kits a kit depends on"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does a kit that requires another kit's system install and restore?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A kit release's .ia/release.json dependencies other than inventarch/language name a kit id and range. Adoption (kit.mjs add), add --from and the restored read root resolve each dependency, transitively and dependencies first, from the kits this plugin carries, then <IA home>/kits, then the dependency's own folder next to the kit; the first whose latest release satisfies the range wins. Its sources must pack to that recorded release, and its archive goes with the language base into the copy's or the workspace's vendor catalog, so ia install --catalog and ia restore --catalog resolve it. A missing dependency, a cycle or two releases of one kit is refused before anything is written."
    rationale "A method kit that mints no words requires the vocabulary kit that does; adoption has to bring that kit along, or the native install refuses."

@decision public-delegate
  meaning
    says "Only through the user-invoked ia:delegate (disable-model-invocation), off by default until the person sets delegation true in ~/.ia/config.json or IA_DELEGATION=1. scripts/delegate.mjs builds a codex exec or claude -p command that gets the IA door (--mcp-config for claude -p, -c mcp_servers.ia for codex exec) and the brief with the packet inline on stdin, sets IA_DELEGATION_DEPTH one higher, refuses when it is already 1 or more, applies a timeout (900 seconds by default, at most 3,600) and runs only with --run."
  work
    title "Delegate across models only on the person's word"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "May the core hand a step to another model's command line?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Only through the user-invoked ia:delegate (disable-model-invocation), off by default until the person sets delegation true in ~/.ia/config.json or IA_DELEGATION=1. scripts/delegate.mjs builds a codex exec or claude -p command that gets the IA door (--mcp-config for claude -p, -c mcp_servers.ia for codex exec) and the brief with the packet inline on stdin, sets IA_DELEGATION_DEPTH one higher, refuses when it is already 1 or more, applies a timeout (900 seconds by default, at most 3,600) and runs only with --run."
    rationale "Cross-model delegation spends another account and reaches outside the session, so the person turns it on and starts it, and one level deep keeps it from fanning out."

@decision public-kit-trust
  meaning
    says "A kit may run code (a handlers.json run script, an onwrite.json command or a checks.json command) only when its publisher, the kit id's namespace, with which a .ia/release.json publisher field must agree, is on the plugin's config/trusted-publishers.json, which ships as inventarch alone, and its sources digest equals its latest release's recorded sources, with .ia/release.json naming that release; a handler's run script must also be a declared asset, except the core's own allowlisted ${PLUGIN} script (decision public-method-advisory-run-points), whose sidecar the digest covers. Any other kit, and a kit with no recorded release, is data only: a handler entry may carry a context text, delivered as additionalContext with no process; its run entries and onwrite.json commands are skipped, its checks are refused with IA-CORE-KIT-UNTRUSTED, and the SessionStart answer names each such kit once, with why. A pre handler of a kit whose publisher is not on the list never blocks. In a governed workspace a trusted pre handler that fails, times out or answers past the output cap refuses the call, and an altered gate refuses every pre-tool call: while a kit whose id names a trusted publisher declares a pre handler whose code the trust check refuses (its bytes are not its recorded release, or its script is not a declared asset), each such call is refused, naming the kit and the fix, which is to reinstall its recorded release with node <ia plugin>/scripts/kit.mjs add --from <release> --apply or to remove .ia/governance.json. Nothing extends the list at run time. Every child the three runners start gets an allowlisted environment (never NODE_OPTIONS or NODE_PATH), its process tree is killed on its deadline, and a hook child keeps at most 256 KiB of stdout and of stderr: hygiene, not a sandbox."
  work
    title "Trust kit code by publisher and recorded digest"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-31"
    ended "2026-10-31"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which kits may run code through handlers.json, onwrite.json and checks.json, and with what around it?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A kit may run code (a handlers.json run script, an onwrite.json command or a checks.json command) only when its publisher, the kit id's namespace, with which a .ia/release.json publisher field must agree, is on the plugin's config/trusted-publishers.json, which ships as inventarch alone, and its sources digest equals its latest release's recorded sources, with .ia/release.json naming that release; a handler's run script must also be a declared asset, except the core's own allowlisted ${PLUGIN} script (decision public-method-advisory-run-points), whose sidecar the digest covers. Any other kit, and a kit with no recorded release, is data only: a handler entry may carry a context text, delivered as additionalContext with no process; its run entries and onwrite.json commands are skipped, its checks are refused with IA-CORE-KIT-UNTRUSTED, and the SessionStart answer names each such kit once, with why. A pre handler of a kit whose publisher is not on the list never blocks. In a governed workspace a trusted pre handler that fails, times out or answers past the output cap refuses the call, and an altered gate refuses every pre-tool call: while a kit whose id names a trusted publisher declares a pre handler whose code the trust check refuses (its bytes are not its recorded release, or its script is not a declared asset), each such call is refused, naming the kit and the fix, which is to reinstall its recorded release with node <ia plugin>/scripts/kit.mjs add --from <release> --apply or to remove .ia/governance.json. Nothing extends the list at run time. Every child the three runners start gets an allowlisted environment (never NODE_OPTIONS or NODE_PATH), its process tree is killed on its deadline, and a hook child keeps at most 256 KiB of stdout and of stderr: hygiene, not a sandbox."
    rationale "An adversarial review of making runtime/local's restricted worker a sandbox for kit handlers found that Node's permission model leaves the network open, lets child processes run unconfined and lets a debugger attach to another of the person's Node processes, so no handler sandbox exists to approve. Trust therefore rests on who published a kit and whether its bytes are the release they recorded, the same for every runner, and until the execution-binding release only InventArch-published kits run code. The list is not extensible at run time, because an agent with a shell can write any setting, environment variable or file in the IA home and so could approve itself. The publisher is a claim, not a signature, and the digest is checked against the kit's own release record and covers its declared entry scripts, not every module they import: this stops drift and other publishers' kits, not a deliberate impersonation, which signed releases will close. An altered gate fails closed (operator ruling, 2026-10-31): a governance kit whose bytes drifted must not leave a governed repository silently ungoverned, and a kit from a publisher that is not on the list cannot block, so installing one never stops a person's work."

@decision public-kits-package
  meaning
    says "@inventarch/public-kits carries the kits the core bundles as one npm package, generated in packages/public-kits/ by node tools/kit.mjs package from plugins/ia/config/kits and kits/index.json. Its committed package.json holds its own semver, raised whenever a carried release changes (consistency --base checks it), and ia.kits: each kit's id, latest version, IA archive and manifest sha256, sources digest and folder; it has no scripts, dependencies, entry points or exports. The built files, gitignored, are archives/<archive>.ia.tgz (each recorded release packed again with the pinned CLI, refused unless it is that release) with archives/<archive>.distribution.json (its manifest), kits/<folder>/ byte for byte, index.json, LICENSE and NOTICE; the kit folders stay because an archive carries no sidecars. package --check is a gate in lefthook, npm run check and the gates workflow. .github/workflows/publish-public-kits.yml, started only by workflow_dispatch from main, checks the package against its sources, refuses a version npm already has, builds it and runs npm publish --provenance --access public under OIDC trusted publishing with no token; running it is the operator's step."
  work
    title "Publish the bundled kits as @inventarch/public-kits"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-31"
    ended "2026-10-31"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How do the default kits reach a door or workspace that has no plugin, and how is that package built and published?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "@inventarch/public-kits carries the kits the core bundles as one npm package, generated in packages/public-kits/ by node tools/kit.mjs package from plugins/ia/config/kits and kits/index.json. Its committed package.json holds its own semver, raised whenever a carried release changes (consistency --base checks it), and ia.kits: each kit's id, latest version, IA archive and manifest sha256, sources digest and folder; it has no scripts, dependencies, entry points or exports. The built files, gitignored, are archives/<archive>.ia.tgz (each recorded release packed again with the pinned CLI, refused unless it is that release) with archives/<archive>.distribution.json (its manifest), kits/<folder>/ byte for byte, index.json, LICENSE and NOTICE; the kit folders stay because an archive carries no sidecars. package --check is a gate in lefthook, npm run check and the gates workflow. .github/workflows/publish-public-kits.yml, started only by workflow_dispatch from main, checks the package against its sources, refuses a version npm already has, builds it and runs npm publish --provenance --access public under OIDC trusted publishing with no token; running it is the operator's step."
    rationale "The operator's rulings rereview-ia-q1-default-kits-package, dec-122 and dec-133 (C7, C7b) send the default kits to a bare door as one npm package that a lock records as one entry, with npm as transport only: the IA engine expands each kit from its archive, so the package must carry the archives and their manifests, by the digests their releases recorded. Generating it from the bundled kits keeps it from drifting, and committing only package.json keeps the claim reviewable without a third copy of every kit's bytes in history."

@decision public-upkeep-agent
  meaning
    says "The core ships the upkeep agent (plugins/ia/agents/upkeep.md, background, isolated in a worktree, model sonnet, a tools allowlist) with its deterministic half scripts/upkeep.mjs. upkeep.mjs stale compares content digests of the inputs a kit's derived.json sidecar declares with the digests recorded in .ia/derived.lock.json, and lists records whose source, cites or grounded-by lines name a changed file; it exits 3 and starts nothing when nothing is stale. The agent acts only on that report and returns a patch from its own worktree: it never edits the caller's tree, commits or pushes, and never overwrites an output a person edited. Limits: staleness is by digest, not meaning; build runs a script the sidecar names only with --allow-code and without the kit trust check handlers take; nothing starts the agent automatically."
  work
    title "Ship the upkeep agent"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Does the core ship the upkeep agent, and what may it do?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The core ships the upkeep agent (plugins/ia/agents/upkeep.md, background, isolated in a worktree, model sonnet, a tools allowlist) with its deterministic half scripts/upkeep.mjs. upkeep.mjs stale compares content digests of the inputs a kit's derived.json sidecar declares with the digests recorded in .ia/derived.lock.json, and lists records whose source, cites or grounded-by lines name a changed file; it exits 3 and starts nothing when nothing is stale. The agent acts only on that report and returns a patch from its own worktree: it never edits the caller's tree, commits or pushes, and never overwrites an output a person edited. Limits: staleness is by digest, not meaning; build runs a script the sidecar names only with --allow-code and without the kit trust check handlers take; nothing starts the agent automatically."
    rationale "Derived outputs and the records that cite changed files drift silently. A digest check costs no model and decides whether an agent is worth starting; confining the agent to a worktree patch keeps it a proposer."

@decision public-door-codex
  meaning
    says "The Codex manifest of the core names the IA door: mcpServers ./.codex-mcp.json, a file holding server ia run as node ./scripts/door.mjs with cwd ./ (Codex takes a contained ./ command path and cwd, and does not expand CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT, so it cannot use the Claude .mcp.json). The launcher is the same scripts/door.mjs, which starts npx -y @inventarch/mcp-door at the plugin's IA pin and sets IA_KITS_DIR itself. tools/validate.py holds the file to exactly that; tools/smoke-codex.mjs starts the door through Codex (mcpServerStatus/list) and requires ia_session, ia_catalogue and ia_method. Kit handler hooks stay off in Codex (task codex-door-and-handlers)."
  work
    title "Ship the IA door to Codex"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the core's IA door reach Codex?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The Codex manifest of the core names the IA door: mcpServers ./.codex-mcp.json, a file holding server ia run as node ./scripts/door.mjs with cwd ./ (Codex takes a contained ./ command path and cwd, and does not expand CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT, so it cannot use the Claude .mcp.json). The launcher is the same scripts/door.mjs, which starts npx -y @inventarch/mcp-door at the plugin's IA pin and sets IA_KITS_DIR itself. tools/validate.py holds the file to exactly that; tools/smoke-codex.mjs starts the door through Codex (mcpServerStatus/list) and requires ia_session, ia_catalogue and ia_method. Kit handler hooks stay off in Codex (task codex-door-and-handlers)."
    rationale "Codex users get the same method packets through the door the Claude plugin ships, without hand-writing a config entry; a separate file keeps the Claude door file exact."

@decision public-release-notes
  meaning
    says "A pull request that changes a plugin's shipped files adds a release note, releases/pending/<id>.json (format inventarch-marketplace.release-note.v1: bump patch, minor, major or none, a title, a summary and the plugins it names), with node tools/release.mjs note, and never bumps the plugin by hand; consistency --base refuses a shipped change unless the plugin's version rose or a note new or changed since the base names it, and release.mjs check, a gate, refuses a malformed note. The release pull request workflow, started only by an operator's dispatch from main, runs release.mjs version --write (the highest bump among each plugin's notes; both manifests, sources.lock.json pluginVersion and the CHANGELOG entry; releases/changesets/<plugin>-<version>.json; the consumed notes removed) and selection.py --write with GITHUB_TOKEN and no dependency install, force-pushes release/next, opens its pull request as a draft or updates the existing pull request without changing its readiness, and dispatches Gates on it. Merging it with a merge commit starts the release workflow, which waits for Gates on that main commit and then creates the <plugin>-v<version> tag and GitHub release. Kit releases keep their own two-commit flow, and publishing @inventarch/public-kits stays its own dispatch."
  work
    title "Release plugins from pending notes through an operator-started release pull request"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does a change to a plugin reach a released version, and who starts a release?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A pull request that changes a plugin's shipped files adds a release note, releases/pending/<id>.json (format inventarch-marketplace.release-note.v1: bump patch, minor, major or none, a title, a summary and the plugins it names), with node tools/release.mjs note, and never bumps the plugin by hand; consistency --base refuses a shipped change unless the plugin's version rose or a note new or changed since the base names it, and release.mjs check, a gate, refuses a malformed note. The release pull request workflow, started only by an operator's dispatch from main, runs release.mjs version --write (the highest bump among each plugin's notes; both manifests, sources.lock.json pluginVersion and the CHANGELOG entry; releases/changesets/<plugin>-<version>.json; the consumed notes removed) and selection.py --write with GITHUB_TOKEN and no dependency install, force-pushes release/next, opens its pull request as a draft or updates the existing pull request without changing its readiness, and dispatches Gates on it. Merging it with a merge commit starts the release workflow, which waits for Gates on that main commit and then creates the <plugin>-v<version> tag and GitHub release. Kit releases keep their own two-commit flow, and publishing @inventarch/public-kits stays its own dispatch."
    rationale "The operator asked for the public repository to release the way inventarch/ia does, with changesets (2026-11-01). Bumping in every pull request made parallel pull requests collide on the version and let a merged change ship unreleased when its bump was forgotten, as the upkeep spike did; a note per change and one release pull request remove both. The release starts only on dispatch, as the inventarch/ia ruling on release automation chose, and the push to release/next and the release pull request stay on the workflow's own token until the dedicated release credential of issue 3 lands. A note pending at the base does not count for a later change, so every shipped change is described by the pull request that made it."

@decision public-main-rulesets
  meaning
    says "Three repository rulesets, kept as JSON under .github/rulesets/ and applied through the GitHub API. main (no bypass) on the default branch: no deletion, no force push, changes only through a pull request with every review thread resolved, stale approvals dismissed, merge commits as the only merge method, and the Gates (ubuntu-latest) and Native verified (ubuntu-latest) checks passing on a branch up to date with main. main-review: a code owner's review (.github/CODEOWNERS), with the operator's account as the only bypass, in pull_request mode, so the operator's and the operator's agents' own pull requests are never blocked. tags (no bypass): no tag may be deleted, moved or force-updated. The repository allows merge commits only, and its default workflow token is read-only."
  work
    title "Protect main and the tags with rulesets"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What protects main and the release tags now that the repository is public?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Three repository rulesets, kept as JSON under .github/rulesets/ and applied through the GitHub API. main (no bypass) on the default branch: no deletion, no force push, changes only through a pull request with every review thread resolved, stale approvals dismissed, merge commits as the only merge method, and the Gates (ubuntu-latest) and Native verified (ubuntu-latest) checks passing on a branch up to date with main. main-review: a code owner's review (.github/CODEOWNERS), with the operator's account as the only bypass, in pull_request mode, so the operator's and the operator's agents' own pull requests are never blocked. tags (no bypass): no tag may be deleted, moved or force-updated. The repository allows merge commits only, and its default workflow token is read-only."
    rationale "The repository went public on 2026-11-01 with main unprotected, and a pull request had already merged with red checks. GitHub never counts an author's own approval and nearly every pull request here is authored by the operator's account, so a code-owner rule without a bypass would block the operator; splitting the review rule into its own ruleset gives that one account a pull_request bypass while CI, the pull request itself and the history rules bind everyone. Merge commits only, because a kit release pins its sources commit and a squash or rebase would rewrite it. A tag, once the release workflow makes it, is what a user can pin, so nothing may move it."

@decision public-account-connector
  meaning
    says "The core ships two MCP servers, both in plugins/ia/.mcp.json: the IA door, server ia (public-door-mcp), and the InventArch account connector, server inventarch: type http, url https://api.inventarch.dev/mcp, oauth.clientId claude-code, oauth.callbackPort 43822 (the pre-registered public Claude Code client of the API's remote MCP, PKCE S256, callbacks http://localhost:43822/callback and http://127.0.0.1:43822/callback). The plugin ships no secret and stores no token; the host runs the OAuth sign-in (/mcp or claude mcp login inventarch). Claude Code names its tools mcp__plugin_ia_inventarch__<tool> (kit_offerings, kit_download). ia:init section 6 uses them after sign-in: it lists the offerings, downloads each licensed kit through scripts/kit-fetch.mjs (the signed URL goes in on stdin, is never printed or stored, and the archive is written only when its SHA-256 equals the offered digest), installs it with kit.mjs add --from and binds as the kit recommends. The Codex manifest is unchanged: Codex still names only the door (.codex-mcp.json), because this catalogue has not qualified an HTTP server with a pre-registered OAuth client there (task codex-account-connector). tools/validate.py admits that pair, exactly, and nothing else."
  work
    title "Bundle the InventArch account connector with the core"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which MCP servers does the core ship, and how does an account connect?"
    decider "Thomas"
    choice "The core ships two MCP servers, both in plugins/ia/.mcp.json: the IA door, server ia (public-door-mcp), and the InventArch account connector, server inventarch: type http, url https://api.inventarch.dev/mcp, oauth.clientId claude-code, oauth.callbackPort 43822 (the pre-registered public Claude Code client of the API's remote MCP, PKCE S256, callbacks http://localhost:43822/callback and http://127.0.0.1:43822/callback). The plugin ships no secret and stores no token; the host runs the OAuth sign-in (/mcp or claude mcp login inventarch). Claude Code names its tools mcp__plugin_ia_inventarch__<tool> (kit_offerings, kit_download). ia:init section 6 uses them after sign-in: it lists the offerings, downloads each licensed kit through scripts/kit-fetch.mjs (the signed URL goes in on stdin, is never printed or stored, and the archive is written only when its SHA-256 equals the offered digest), installs it with kit.mjs add --from and binds as the kit recommends. The Codex manifest is unchanged: Codex still names only the door (.codex-mcp.json), because this catalogue has not qualified an HTTP server with a pre-registered OAuth client there (task codex-account-connector). tools/validate.py admits that pair, exactly, and nothing else."
    rationale "Nobody should hand-run claude mcp add, an admin CLI or an environment edit: signing in once should be enough to set everything up. The API has one pre-registered public client for Claude Code and no dynamic client registration, and Claude Code accepts that client in a plugin's .mcp.json (claude plugin validate passes it), so the plugin can carry the whole connection without a secret. This supersedes public-door-mcp, which made the door the only MCP server. Options considered: (1) Keep the single IA door and tell the user to run claude mcp add by hand; (2) Bundle the account connector with the Claude Code OAuth client in the core; (3) Ship an installer script that registers the server and the client for the user. Chosen: (2)."
  relationships
    supersedes @decision public-door-mcp

@decision public-account-pointer
  meaning
    says "At session startup only, once per session, never blocking, in one line, the core points the user to sign in to InventArch through the bundled connector: run /mcp and choose inventarch (or claude mcp login inventarch). It makes no network call, reads no credentials and writes nothing. An account counts as present when a design binding is set in the environment or the host lists an inventarch MCP server the user added by hand in its documented local configuration. Limits: a sign-in to the bundled connector is kept with the host's credentials, which the core never opens, so after a sign-in the pointer still shows once at startup, says to ignore it when already signed in, and never blocks. It is suppressed by 'accountOffer': false in ~/.ia/config.json, by IA_NO_ACCOUNT_OFFER=1, or by a non-empty CI."
  work
    title "Point at the connector sign-in instead of teaching claude mcp add"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What does the session-start account offer say now that the core bundles the connector?"
    decider "Thomas"
    choice "At session startup only, once per session, never blocking, in one line, the core points the user to sign in to InventArch through the bundled connector: run /mcp and choose inventarch (or claude mcp login inventarch). It makes no network call, reads no credentials and writes nothing. An account counts as present when a design binding is set in the environment or the host lists an inventarch MCP server the user added by hand in its documented local configuration. Limits: a sign-in to the bundled connector is kept with the host's credentials, which the core never opens, so after a sign-in the pointer still shows once at startup, says to ignore it when already signed in, and never blocks. It is suppressed by 'accountOffer': false in ~/.ia/config.json, by IA_NO_ACCOUNT_OFFER=1, or by a non-empty CI."
    rationale "With the connector bundled, the offer no longer teaches a command; it points at the sign-in. Calling the API to learn whether the user is signed in would break the no-network rule and would not help a first-time user; reading the host's credentials file would break the public-core-hooks promise that the hook reads no credentials, so a sign-in through the connector is not detected and the pointer stays one line that may be ignored. This supersedes public-account-offer. Options considered: (1) Keep the single IA door and tell the user to run claude mcp add by hand; (2) Point at the sign-in the bundled connector already offers; (3) Detect the sign-in by calling the API. Chosen: (2)."
  relationships
    supersedes @decision public-account-offer

# The IA 3.6.0 migration (2026-11-08). Release N migrates on the 3.5.x pins; release N+1 moves to 3.6.0.

@decision public-kit-lookup-installed
  meaning
    says "The core still finds a kit to read in its own config/kits, then in <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/, and keeps public-kit-lookup's verbs (list, add, bind, upgrade, repin, rebind and check in plugins/ia/scripts/kit.mjs; pack, bundle, index and convert in tools/kit.mjs; every write validates first and previews unless --apply) and its kits/index.json broadcast. A key the repository binds runs only a kit the repository's own .ia/distributions.lock.json installs (a packages row or a package entry): this plugin's resolver and checks (resolve.mjs, check.mjs) enforce it from release N, and the IA door enforces it too."
  work
    title "Run a bound kit only when the repository installs it"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where does the core find a kit, and which kit may a bound key run?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "The core still finds a kit to read in its own config/kits, then in <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/, and keeps public-kit-lookup's verbs (list, add, bind, upgrade, repin, rebind and check in plugins/ia/scripts/kit.mjs; pack, bundle, index and convert in tools/kit.mjs; every write validates first and previews unless --apply) and its kits/index.json broadcast. A key the repository binds runs only a kit the repository's own .ia/distributions.lock.json installs (a packages row or a package entry): this plugin's resolver and checks (resolve.mjs, check.mjs) enforce it from release N, and the IA door enforces it too."
    rationale "IA 3.6.0's door refuses IA-CORE-KIT-NOT-INSTALLED for a bound kit the lock does not install, so a carried or IA-home copy no longer stands in for a bound key. The plugin's own resolver applies the same rule, so that the two routes agree."
  relationships
    supersedes @decision public-kit-lookup

@decision public-method-report-gate
  meaning
    says "The ia plugin ships two advisory method reports, exit-evidence.mjs and method-gate.mjs, that read a repository's work records against the principle method-claims-within-evidence and the provisional-foundation ceiling; they report and never block."
  work
    title "Ship the method reports as a report-only gate"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-09"
    ended "2026-11-09"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the ia plugin report whether closed tasks carry exit evidence and stay within the ceiling, and what does each script's exit code mean?"
    options ["A advisory reports that exit 0 for findings, with exit 2 for usage errors and for a task that reads unknown", "B reports that exit 1 on a finding", "C no report until a blocking step exists"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A exit-evidence.mjs reports each closed task's evidence from the observation records that name it, and method-gate.mjs runs it with ceiling.mjs; both only report, and the rules are named ceiling and exit-evidence. Positions, first match wins: refused (a current refusal), unknown (the task or an observation could not be read: not evaluated), evidenced (a current success), exempt (an observation whose interpretation.basis is nothing-to-run: the author's declaration, not verified, listed and not a finding), inconclusive (a current inconclusive verdict), stale (a success at another revision, or with no revision recorded) and no-evidence. An observation is current when its evidence.subject-revision equals the task's own digest; a refusal at another revision, or with none recorded, is not current and is listed as information. Reading chosen here, which the principle method-claims-within-evidence does not state: a current refusal stands beside an exemption and beside an unreadable observation, and an exemption needs no currency because it is only a declaration. ia next is looser: it calls a task evidenced on one current success whatever else was observed. Exit codes: exit-evidence.mjs exits 0 whenever it produced a report, findings included, and each line starts with the capitalised position, so exit 0 cannot be read as clear; exit 2 means a usage error, an IA refusal, a missing CLI, a task that is not admitted or not a task, or, with --task, a task that reads unknown, which mirrors ceiling.mjs: exit 2 from the standalone script means unknown, not a finding and never clear; it never exits 1 and it refuses --strict. method-gate.mjs maps every unknown or refused result to not evaluated and exits 0, prints the number of rules evaluated, and exits 2 only for bad usage, a --root that is not a directory, and --strict; a nonzero exit waits for a later step that makes the reports blocking. Not shipped in this change: durable check outputs, because their only reader is a later report and keeping them raises questions this change does not settle (up to 64 MiB per stream, possible secrets in the output, a new directory for every run, which run to keep). This catalogue's own CI runs the method gate as a report only, in a separate workflow (public-method-gate-ci); the plugin README shows how a consumer's CI can. nothing-to-run is this catalogue's own basis id, outside the learning-system basis set; it is not to be authored until learning-system admits it."
    rationale "The principle is not enforced by anything yet, so a first report has to be honest about what it did not evaluate and cannot be mistaken for a pass: every line starts with its position, a rule it could not evaluate is printed in capitals, and a run that evaluated nothing says so. A nonzero exit on a finding would turn a report into a block before the reports are trusted on real repositories."

@decision public-method-gate-ci
  meaning
    says "This catalogue runs the ia plugin's method gate on its own work records in a separate, report-only workflow, and carries observation records for the closed tasks its public records support."
  work
    title "Run the method gate on this catalogue's records as a report"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-09"
    ended "2026-11-09"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Does this catalogue run the method gate on its own records, how, and what evidence do its closed tasks carry?"
    options ["A a separate report-only workflow", "B a step in the Gates workflow", "C no run in this catalogue"]
    decider "The merge of the pull request that adds this record, reviewed by a code owner"
    choice "A a separate workflow, .github/workflows/method-gate.yml, with one Ubuntu job that restores the workspace (npm ci, then npm run kits:install, because a fresh checkout is not an admitted IA workspace), passes every plan the records author by its full identity, and prints method-gate.mjs's report to the job summary. It runs on pull requests into main, on pushes to main and on dispatch, with a read-only token. It exits 0 on findings; a setup failure turns it red; and when the report says a rule or task was NOT EVALUATED or UNKNOWN it raises a warning, because exit 0 alone does not show that everything was checked; method-gate.mjs asks the CLI to validate the workspace first and, unless it is admitted (refused, any other status, or validate cannot run), reads both rules NOT EVALUATED (workspace not admitted) instead of reading every closed task as having no evidence. It is not one of the required checks (Gates (ubuntu-latest) and Native verified (ubuntu-latest) stay as they are), and making it block is later work. The closed tasks carry the evidence the rule reads: 16 of the 18 closed tasks have one observation each in .ia/src/evidence.ia, with origin check or run, verdict success, interpretation.basis inference and the public pull request, CI run, tag or npm record as locator. They were recorded by an AI agent from those public records, not reviewed by a maintainer, and each states its limits; the two host-gate tasks rest on the committed Codex smoke report only, because no public record holds the claude plugin validate --strict output. cli-3-6-0-install-migration and cli-3-6-0-own-lock stay without evidence, because each names a deliverable this repository does not hold (a test of the real doors, and a kept packet). The gate reads 18 closed tasks: 16 evidenced, 0 exempt, 2 no evidence. No task is exempt, because an exemption is only a declaration. Durable evidence bundles are deferred: the observations' bundle and digest fields read not retained, so the language admits the records but the learning-system consumer cannot read them. The workspace also composes learning-system, the owner of the observation word. Adopted by the merge of the pull request that adds this record, which a code owner reviews."
    rationale "A separate file keeps the required Gates job and the tests that pin its steps unchanged, and a job that is not required can be slow or fail without blocking a merge. The catalogue authors the records the gate reads, so running it here shows what a consumer sees, including the case where it checked less than it says. An observation that claimed more than its public record shows would make the gate read clear when it is not, so each states only what its record shows and a task whose deliverable is absent stays a finding."

@decision public-method-advisory-run-points
  meaning
    says "The ia-method kit's onwrite.json and handlers.json run the core's scripts/method-advise.mjs, which reports the ceiling and the exit evidence of the tasks in the .ia records just written or still uncommitted, as advisory output that blocks nothing."
  work
    title "Run the method reports as advisory hooks from the method kit"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-10"
    ended "2026-11-10"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How do the method reports run where the records change, without a kit shipping code and without blocking anything?"
    options ["A advisory hooks that run one allowlisted script of the core", "B a script shipped inside the kit", "C a context reminder only"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A: the method kit's two sidecars, onwrite.json and handlers.json, run one script of the core, scripts/method-advise.mjs, as advisory hooks that block nothing: after a Write, Edit or MultiEdit of a .ia file (onwrite.json, extension .ia, no marker; this path alone covers file writes, so no handlers.json entry names Write or Edit and no write is advised twice), after a Bash or PowerShell call, and at a resumed or compacted session (handlers.json, tool post and session). The script runs the ceiling and exit-evidence reports for the tasks in the changed or uncommitted .ia records under .ia/src (the one just written, or those still uncommitted; in a tracked file only the records that differ from HEAD, and a removed or changed observation puts the task it was about in scope; a new file whole), at most 2 from a written file and 3 per shell or session call, after asking the CLI to admit the workspace, and prints not evaluated instead of findings when it does not. It states how many tasks it evaluated, how many it did not and why, how many came from the cache, and how many sit in unchanged records it did not examine, and says so when git failed and a file was read whole; it prints a task it could not evaluate as not evaluated and never as clear, and the same for a .ia record inside .ia/src that it refuses to read (over 1 MB, unreadable); it never downloads a CLI (it needs @inventarch/cli installed in the repository, or IA_CLI_PATH); each read gets 5 seconds and the run 18. A small cache under the IA home, in a folder named advice, holds the last result per task: a finding is reported once, again after a resume or compact, and kept an hour; a clean result is kept 10 minutes; a not-evaluated result is said once, again after a resume or compact, and kept 5 minutes, and is tried again at once when a CLI can be found; a different file content, git HEAD, plugin version, kit version or CLI starts over. Handler run form: a handlers.json run of exactly ${PLUGIN}/scripts/method-advise.mjs, an allowlist of literal names in lib/handlers.mjs (PLUGIN_RUNS) and not a pattern, is accepted on session and tool post handlers and refused on tool pre, prompt and stop handlers; it takes no arguments, runs only when that script is the plugin's own file once links are followed, only for a trusted kit (public-kit-trust) and only inside an IA workspace, so a repository with no .ia starts no process. A core that predates the form skips such a kit with a reason. Not shipped: any blocking, a pre, stop or prompt handler, a whole-plan run in a hook (scripts/method-gate.mjs is that report), judgement-based assessment and durable receipts. A closed task that was committed with no evidence is not reported by a hook, and a change to a spec or decision does not re-check the tasks that cite it; a removed record is covered only when it was the subject of an observation, which puts the task it was about in scope. The full report is the only complete check, and a hook's silence is not a clear result."
    rationale "The exit-evidence and ceiling rules were only reports an agent had to remember to run; running them where the records change puts the finding next to the work, while a hook budget of seconds rules out the whole plan, so the scope is the records that changed and is always stated. The kit ships no script, because a fetched release carries no executables and the reports already live in the core; an allowlist of one literal name keeps a trusted kit from running any other script of the core. A skipped check must not stand for a pass, so every case that could not run is printed, and nothing here blocks."

# The move to IA 3.8.0 (2026-11-13): one release. Kits declare the language base they are proven on.

@decision public-cli-3-8-0
  meaning
    says "Exactly @inventarch/cli 3.8.0 and @inventarch/mcp-door 3.8.0, in one release. The 3.8.0 CLI carries every published 3.x language base and its newest is 3.3.0; the kits stay locked on inventarch/language 3.2.0 (archive 7b36e4b4), which it carries unchanged, and the qualified runtime cohort names that locked base. Every kit is re-cut with it and declares inventarch/language ^3.0.0, the minimum ia floor --verify proves."
  work
    title "Pin @inventarch/cli 3.8.0"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-13"
    ended "2026-11-13"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which IA CLI do the catalogue and its kits target after the 3.8.0 release?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Exactly @inventarch/cli 3.8.0 and @inventarch/mcp-door 3.8.0, in one release. The 3.8.0 CLI carries every published 3.x language base and its newest is 3.3.0; the kits stay locked on inventarch/language 3.2.0 (archive 7b36e4b4), which it carries unchanged, and the qualified runtime cohort names that locked base. Every kit is re-cut with it and declares inventarch/language ^3.0.0, the minimum ia floor --verify proves."
    rationale "One exact CLI keeps every packed archive reproducible. From 3.7.0 a CLI carries every published 3.x base, so a kit can declare the minimum it is proven on without locking its users to one CLI release; the lock row stays on 3.2.0 so CLIs up to 3.6.0 still restore the kits. It supersedes public-cli-3-6-0, which is kept with InventArch's other work records, outside this repository."

@decision public-gemini-and-antigravity-hosts
  meaning
    says "Ship deterministic first-class host bundles for Google Antigravity (hosts/antigravity/ia, registered via .agents/plugins.json) and Gemini CLI (hosts/gemini/ia), backed by agy-hook.mjs and gemini-hook.mjs over the core's bootstrap and kit handler runners, with headless gemini --approval-mode plan -p support in delegate.mjs."
  work
    title "Support Google Antigravity and Gemini CLI as first-class hosts"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-15"
    ended "2026-11-15"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the public catalogue deliver the ia plugin, its MCP door, its account connector and its lifecycle hooks to Google Antigravity and Gemini CLI without duplicating authored source?"
    options ["A generate hosts/antigravity/ia and hosts/gemini/ia deterministically from plugins/ia via tools/build-host-plugins.mjs, with host-native manifests, subagent frontmatter and hook adapters", "B hand-maintain separate plugin trees per host", "C ship instructions and MCP snippets only, without native plugins or hooks"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A generate hosts/antigravity/ia and hosts/gemini/ia deterministically from plugins/ia via tools/build-host-plugins.mjs, with host-native manifests, subagent frontmatter and hook adapters"
    rationale "Antigravity requires a clean root plugin.json, mcp_config.json, root hooks.json, rules/AGENTS.md and Antigravity subagent tool names, while Gemini CLI requires gemini-extension.json, hooks/hooks.json with millisecond timeouts, GEMINI.md and Gemini CLI tool names. Deterministic generation from plugins/ia keeps one authored source of truth, wires full lifecycle parity (including PostToolUse buffered into PLUGIN_DATA and flushed on PostInvocation in Antigravity, and strict single-JSON stdout in Gemini CLI), and adds --to gemini to scripts/delegate.mjs."

# Public base vocabulary kits and steward hygiene (2026-11-18)

@decision public-base-kits-approval-and-repo
  meaning
    says "The public catalogue bundles two Apache-2.0 public base vocabulary kits alongside the three lane defaults and publishes all five in @inventarch/public-kits: inventarch/ia-approval-kit 0.1.0 (approval-system: approver, approval-rule, docket and approval, with the zero-dependency offline Ed25519 + RFC 8785 JCS check-seal verifier) and inventarch/ia-repo-kit 0.1.0 (repo-system: repository, layer, package and area, with the rank-independent dependency-role grammar, spec citation consent rows and offline check-layers verifier)."
  work
    title "Ship ia-approval and ia-repo as bundled public base kits"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-18"
    ended "2026-11-18"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where do the public approval and repository-structure vocabularies and their offline verifiers ship?"
    options ["A bundle kits/ia-approval (approval-system) and kits/ia-repo (repo-system) in inventarch/marketplace under Apache-2.0 and @inventarch/public-kits", "B keep approval and repository-structure words in private kits"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A bundle kits/ia-approval 0.1.0 (inventarch/ia-approval-kit, approval-system) and kits/ia-repo 0.1.0 (inventarch/ia-repo-kit, repo-system) in inventarch/marketplace under Apache-2.0, carry them in plugins/ia/config/kits and @inventarch/public-kits alongside ia-method, ia-kb and ia-authoring, and ship their offline Node built-in checks (check-seal and check-layers) with zero external npm dependencies and zero private corpus material."
    rationale "Only public kits mint words that customer and hosted workflows share offline. Shipping approval-system and repo-system in inventarch/marketplace and @inventarch/public-kits lets adopting repositories restore, validate and run check-seal and check-layers 100% offline in CI without vended tarballs or credentials, while keeping the three lane default bindings unchanged."

@decision public-system-steward-hygiene
  meaning
    says "New public base systems in this catalogue name <system> with a uniform @agent <system>-steward and travelling @mandate <system>-stewardship scoped to the system's folder, and the catalogue's own @workspace inventarch-marketplace composes agent-system with @agent inventarch-marketplace-steward and @mandate inventarch-marketplace-stewardship."
  work
    title "Uniform steward and stewardship mandate hygiene"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-18"
    ended "2026-11-18"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How do new public base systems and the marketplace workspace declare stewardship and bounded mandates?"
    options ["A declare uniform @agent <system>-steward and @mandate <system>-stewardship on each new base system and steward + mandate on inventarch-marketplace", "B declare steward agents without stewardship mandates"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A each new public base system (approval-system, repo-system, slack-system, design-base-system, product-base-system) declares steward @agent <system>-steward and @mandate <system>-stewardship with moves [Observation, Verification, Synthesis] and covers ['.ia/src/systems/<system>/'] inside system.ia so stewardship travels in the packed archive without referencing the seat workspace, while @workspace inventarch-marketplace declares steward @agent inventarch-marketplace-steward and @mandate inventarch-marketplace-stewardship."
    rationale "Paired steward and stewardship mandate records make system ownership and non-execution bounds explicit in every packed base kit and in the catalogue's own workspace without rewriting immutable historical releases of earlier kits."

@decision public-base-kits-slack-design-product
  meaning
    says "The public catalogue bundles the remaining three Apache-2.0 public base vocabulary kits alongside ia-approval, ia-repo and the three lane defaults, completing the eight-kit @inventarch/public-kits v1 roster: inventarch/ia-slack-base-kit 0.1.0 (slack-system: slack-channel, the five public channel governance rules and offline check-slack-channels), inventarch/ia-design-base-kit 0.1.0 (design-base-system: design-token, level, part, slot, pattern and recipe without colliding with floor @template, plus offline check-design-patterns) and inventarch/ia-product-base-kit 0.1.0 (product-base-system: prd with consented spec/decision/plan/docket/approval traceability and offline check-prds)."
  work
    title "Ship ia-slack-base, ia-design-base and ia-product-base as bundled public base kits"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-18"
    ended "2026-11-18"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where do the Slack space settings, Atomic Design pattern/recipe and Product Requirement Document base vocabularies and their offline verifiers ship?"
    options ["A bundle kits/ia-slack-base, kits/ia-design-base and kits/ia-product-base in inventarch/marketplace under Apache-2.0 and @inventarch/public-kits", "B keep Slack channel settings, pattern/recipe and PRD words in private kits"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A bundle kits/ia-slack-base 0.1.0 (inventarch/ia-slack-base-kit, slack-system), kits/ia-design-base 0.1.0 (inventarch/ia-design-base-kit, design-base-system) and kits/ia-product-base 0.1.0 (inventarch/ia-product-base-kit, product-base-system) in inventarch/marketplace under Apache-2.0, carry all eight public kits in plugins/ia/config/kits and @inventarch/public-kits, and ship their zero-dependency offline verifiers (check-slack-channels, check-design-patterns and check-prds) with zero live credentials and zero private corpus material."
    rationale "Completing the eight-kit public base roster lets workspace, design and product repositories restore, validate and verify Slack channel rules, atomic UI hierarchies, sealed pattern/recipe lowering contracts and PRD traceability 100% offline in CI without vended archives or hosted credentials, while keeping live bridges and proprietary multi-step methods outside the public base."

@decision public-check-windows-package-managers
  meaning
    says "On Windows, plugins/ia/scripts/check.mjs resolves bare npm, npx, pnpm and yarn argv[0] entries to their native .exe binary or Node JS entrypoint under process.execPath while preserving shell: false for every declared check."
  work
    title "Resolve Windows npm, npx, pnpm and yarn check shims without a shell"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-18"
    ended "2026-11-18"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How should scripts/check.mjs execute declared checks whose argv starts with npm, npx, pnpm or yarn on Windows without weakening the no-shell guarantee?"
    options ["A resolve bare npm, npx, pnpm and yarn on Windows to their .exe or JS entrypoint under process.execPath with shell: false", "B spawn .cmd shims through cmd.exe with shell: true", "C require every kit to hardcode Node and package-manager install paths"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A when process.platform is win32 and argv[0] is npm, npx, pnpm or yarn, resolve a standalone .exe or the underlying .cmd shim's JS entrypoint (or the entrypoint beside process.execPath) and spawn it directly with process.execPath and shell: false; pass every other command and non-Windows platform through unchanged."
    rationale "Direct execution under process.execPath fixes spawn ENOENT on Windows (issue #32) while keeping cmd.exe completely out of the execution path so environment-variable expansion and batch quote mangling remain impossible."

# Canonical custody of systems, routing specifications and kits (2026-11-19).

@decision public-systems-and-kits-custody
  meaning
    says "Separate vocabulary systems, kit routing specifications and kit methodology bodies across the open framework, the public marketplace, hosted platform execution and private estates. Locally authored systems need no external pin. Adopted systems are pinned by exact version or revision and applicable digests; with their required inputs and a compatible verifier present, validation can run offline; public starter and base vocabulary kits ship in inventarch/marketplace; proprietary kit bodies execute strictly on the InventArch API and isolated execution pods rather than downloading to client machines or vendoring into repositories, while adopters pin their required systems and a lightweight routing specification locally. Organisation-specific API, MCP and authentication bindings stay in private estates."
  work
    title "Separate locally pinned systems and routing specs from hosted proprietary kit bodies"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-19"
    ended "2026-11-19"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What is downloaded and pinned locally versus executed remotely for kits, and what belongs in inventarch/ia, inventarch/marketplace and private repositories?"
    options ["A separate systems (local source or exact adopted pins), kit routing specifications (local stub) and kit bodies (public starter and base kits in marketplace; proprietary kit bodies hosted-only on the InventArch API), while keeping private API/MCP/auth glue out of marketplace", "B download full proprietary kit archives into ~/.ia/kits or vendor/ia on client machines", "C keep both systems and proprietary kits remote-only with no local vocabulary pins"]
    decider "Owner"
    choice "A separate systems (local source or exact adopted pins), kit routing specifications (local stub) and kit bodies (public starter and base kits in marketplace; proprietary kit bodies hosted-only on the InventArch API), while keeping private API/MCP/auth glue out of marketplace"
    rationale "Earlier choices flip-flopped because vocabulary (@system and @schema) and methodology (playbooks, rubrics and roles) travelled as one archive. The floor reserves its words. For other registrations, the unique highest authority band wins, lower bands may be shadowed, and highest-band ties block the word. Pinning fixes inputs, not word meaning across accounts. Under the target custody model a local harness agent also needs a lightweight routing specification stating when to call a hosted kit and what inputs apply, without seeing how the proprietary method works internally. Keeping proprietary kit bodies strictly on the InventArch API and execution pods protects proprietary methodology while preserving offline validation. plugins/ia 1.x retains public-account-connector's transitional kit_download path pending the hosted custody migration tracked in inventarch/ia-kb#285. This decision does not establish hosted availability."

# Public punt runner and Antigravity local swarm plan sync (2026-11-26).

@decision public-punt-bridge
  meaning
    says "The ia plugin ships two zero-dependency helper scripts for public-kit task punting and Antigravity local swarm plan projection: plugins/ia/scripts/punt.mjs validates PublicPuntEnvelopeV1 payloads against the eight-kit public catalogue allowlist, enforces single-hash subjectSha256 parity with subjectLabel, resolves methods through scripts/resolve.mjs with fallbackUnbound to inventarch/ia-method-kit#method-operate on IA-CORE-NO-WORKSPACE only (refusing silent fallback on IA-CORE-NO-METHOD), renders repoVerificationCommands in preview-by-default mode unless --execute-repo-commands runs them shell-free under an allowlisted environment and symlink-checked --cwd, verifies declaredKitChecks receipts and emits WitnessedPuntReceiptV1; plugins/ia/scripts/agy-swarm-sync.mjs validates SwarmControlViewV1 plan fields (goal.title 4 to 8 words, goal.line at most 12 words, checkpoints/roster or tasks, and 1 to 6 threads) and atomically writes plan.json to an explicit --swarm-dir without touching builtin directories or hardcoded OS paths."
  work
    title "Ship public punt runner and Antigravity swarm plan sync in plugins/ia"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-26"
    ended "2026-11-26"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the public ia plugin execute punted public-kit work envelopes and project swarm plans to Antigravity without bundling proprietary kits or hardcoded host paths?"
    options ["A ship plugins/ia/scripts/punt.mjs (public-kit allowlist, single-hash subject parity, unbound fallback on IA-CORE-NO-WORKSPACE only, preview-by-default repo commands unless --execute-repo-commands, shell-free verification receipts) and plugins/ia/scripts/agy-swarm-sync.mjs (explicit --swarm-dir atomic plan.json writer supporting checkpoints/roster or tasks)", "B bundle proprietary orchestration kits into plugins/ia", "C write directly into host builtin or hardcoded user directories"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A ship plugins/ia/scripts/punt.mjs (public-kit allowlist, single-hash subject parity, unbound fallback on IA-CORE-NO-WORKSPACE only, preview-by-default repo commands unless --execute-repo-commands, shell-free verification receipts) and plugins/ia/scripts/agy-swarm-sync.mjs (explicit --swarm-dir atomic plan.json writer supporting checkpoints/roster or tasks)"
    rationale "Keeping the public plugin strictly limited to allowlisted public kits, explicit directory arguments, preview-by-default command handling and shell-free verification preserves custody boundaries, fails closed on missing method bindings and maintains offline reproducibility while enabling cross-host punted execution and local Antigravity swarm overview rendering."
